   "metadata": {},
   "source": [
    "# Financial News Sentiment Analysis using FinBERT\n",
    "\n",
    "Hey! This is my mini-project where I used **FinBERT** (a BERT model already trained on financial text) to check whether Indian financial news headlines are **Positive**, **Negative**, or **Neutral**.\n",
    "\n",
    "I'm using the `IndianFinancialNews.csv` dataset which has around 50K+ headlines. My plan for this notebook:\n",
    "\n",
    "1. Load the data and clean it up a bit\n",
    "2. Run FinBERT on every headline to get a sentiment label + confidence score\n",
    "3. Do some basic EDA - see how sentiment is distributed and how it changes over time\n",
    "4. Make a few charts to visualize everything\n",
    "5. Write down a couple of insights and what I'd improve if I had more time\n"
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "markdown",
   "id": "2bb9717a",
   "metadata": {},
   "source": [
    "## Step 1: Installing and importing what I need"
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "code",
   "execution_count": null,
   "id": "c9cb39fc",
   "metadata": {},
   "outputs": [],
   "source": [
    "!pip install pandas matplotlib seaborn requests tqdm -q\n"
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "code",
   "execution_count": null,
   "id": "d9afd908",
   "metadata": {},
   "outputs": [],
   "source": [
    "import pandas as pd\n",
    "import matplotlib.pyplot as plt\n",
    "import seaborn as sns\n",
    "import requests\n",
    "import time\n",
    "from tqdm.auto import tqdm\n",
    "\n",
    "sns.set_style(\"whitegrid\")\n"
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "markdown",
   "id": "0146b65c",
   "metadata": {},
   "source": [
    "## Step 2: Load the data\n",
    "\n",
    "Just reading in the CSV. It has three columns - `Date`, `Title`, and `Description`. For this project I'm mainly working with the headline (`Title`).\n"
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "code",
   "execution_count": null,
   "id": "303c8929",
   "metadata": {},
   "outputs": [],
   "source": [
    "df = pd.read_csv(\"IndianFinancialNews.csv\")\n",
    "print(df.shape)\n",
    "df.head()\n"
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "markdown",
   "id": "b74bb23e",
   "metadata": {},
   "source": [
    "## Step 3: Cleaning the data\n",
    "\n",
    "Nothing fancy here - just getting rid of duplicate headlines, dropping rows where the headline is missing, and making sure the date column is actually a date. I'm keeping the text mostly as-is (no lowercasing, no removing punctuation) because FinBERT is a transformer model and works better on natural, readable sentences rather than \"cleaned\" text.\n"
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "code",
   "execution_count": null,
   "id": "3378e0fc",
   "metadata": {},
   "outputs": [],
   "source": [
    "df = df.drop_duplicates(subset=[\"Title\"])\n",
    "df = df.dropna(subset=[\"Title\"])\n",
    "df[\"Date\"] = pd.to_datetime(df[\"Date\"], errors=\"coerce\")\n",
    "df = df.dropna(subset=[\"Date\"])\n",
    "df = df.reset_index(drop=True)\n",
    "\n",
    "print(\"Rows after cleaning:\", df.shape[0])\n",
    "df.head()\n"
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "markdown",
   "id": "ce8357f6",
   "metadata": {},
   "source": [
    "## Step 4: Running FinBERT (via the Hugging Face Inference API)\n",
    "\n",
    "Instead of downloading the whole FinBERT model onto my machine, I'm just calling Hugging Face's hosted **Inference API** over the internet. Their servers run the model, I just send headlines and get sentiment back - no `torch`/`transformers` download needed.\n",
    "\n",
    "To use it you need a free Hugging Face account and an access token:\n",
    "1. Sign up at huggingface.co\n",
    "2. Go to Settings -> Access Tokens -> create a new token (\"read\" access is enough)\n",
    "3. Paste it below\n",
    "\n",
    "Note: the free API sometimes takes a few extra seconds to \"wake up\" the model the first time it's called, and it can be rate-limited for very large datasets - I've added a small retry/wait loop to handle that.\n"
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "code",
   "execution_count": null,
   "id": "94089070",
   "metadata": {},
   "outputs": [],
   "source": [
    "HF_TOKEN = \"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"   # <-- paste your token here\n",
    "\n",
    "API_URL = \"https://api-inference.huggingface.co/models/ProsusAI/finbert\"\n",
    "headers = {\"Authorization\": f\"Bearer {HF_TOKEN}\"}\n",
    "\n",
    "\n",
    "def get_sentiment(texts):\n",
    "    \"\"\"Sends a small batch of headlines to the FinBERT API and returns the results.\"\"\"\n",
    "    response = requests.post(API_URL, headers=headers, json={\"inputs\": texts, \"options\": {\"wait_for_model\": True}})\n",
    "    return response.json()\n",
    "\n",
    "\n",
    "# quick test on a couple of headlines to make sure it's working\n",
    "get_sentiment([\"Markets rally as inflation cools\", \"Company shares crash after fraud allegations\"])\n"
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "code",
   "execution_count": null,
   "id": "b55dd6aa",
   "metadata": {},
   "outputs": [],
   "source": [
    "# The free Inference API is a lot slower than running the model locally, and can rate-limit big requests.\n",
    "# So for this demo I'm scoring a sample of headlines instead of all 50K+ - bump this up (or set to None\n",
    "# to run on everything) if you have more time or a paid Inference Endpoint.\n",
    "SAMPLE_SIZE = 2000\n",
    "\n",
    "if SAMPLE_SIZE:\n",
    "    df = df.sample(SAMPLE_SIZE, random_state=42).reset_index(drop=True)\n",
    "\n",
    "headlines = df[\"Title\"].tolist()\n",
    "batch_size = 10   # keeping batches small so the API doesn't complain\n",
    "\n",
    "labels, scores = [], []\n",
    "\n",
    "for i in tqdm(range(0, len(headlines), batch_size)):\n",
    "    batch = headlines[i:i + batch_size]\n",
    "    result = get_sentiment(batch)\n",
    "\n",
    "    # if the API sends back an error instead of predictions, wait a bit and try once more\n",
    "    if isinstance(result, dict) and \"error\" in result:\n",
    "        time.sleep(5)\n",
    "        result = get_sentiment(batch)\n",
    "\n",
    "    for prediction in result:\n",
    "        best = max(prediction, key=lambda x: x[\"score\"])\n",
    "        labels.append(best[\"label\"])\n",
    "        scores.append(best[\"score\"])\n",
    "\n",
    "df[\"Sentiment\"] = labels\n",
    "df[\"Confidence\"] = scores\n",
    "\n",
    "df[[\"Title\", \"Sentiment\", \"Confidence\"]].head()\n"
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "code",
   "execution_count": null,
   "id": "b3064f89",
   "metadata": {},
   "outputs": [],
   "source": [
    "# saving this so I don't have to run FinBERT again every time I reopen the notebook\n",
    "df.to_csv(\"IndianFinancialNews_with_sentiment.csv\", index=False)\n",
    "print(\"Saved!\")\n"
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "markdown",
   "id": "0662d439",
   "metadata": {},
   "source": [
    "## Step 5: Exploring the sentiment (EDA)"
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "markdown",
   "id": "66edd721",
   "metadata": {},
   "source": [
    "### How many headlines fall into each sentiment?"
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "code",
   "execution_count": null,
   "id": "d13c79ac",
   "metadata": {},
   "outputs": [],
   "source": [
    "counts = df[\"Sentiment\"].value_counts()\n",
    "percent = df[\"Sentiment\"].value_counts(normalize=True) * 100\n",
    "\n",
    "print(counts)\n",
    "print()\n",
    "print(percent.round(1))\n"
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "code",
   "execution_count": null,
   "id": "d70f2fd4",
   "metadata": {},
   "outputs": [],
   "source": [
    "colors = {\"positive\": \"#2ca02c\", \"negative\": \"#d62728\", \"neutral\": \"#7f7f7f\"}\n",
    "\n",
    "plt.figure(figsize=(6, 4))\n",
    "sns.countplot(data=df, x=\"Sentiment\", order=counts.index,\n",
    "              palette=[colors[s] for s in counts.index])\n",
    "plt.title(\"Sentiment Distribution of Headlines\")\n",
    "plt.ylabel(\"Number of Headlines\")\n",
    "plt.show()\n"
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "code",
   "execution_count": null,
   "id": "1a8ad209",
   "metadata": {},
   "outputs": [],
   "source": [
    "counts.plot.pie(autopct=\"%1.1f%%\", colors=[colors[s] for s in counts.index], figsize=(5, 5))\n",
    "plt.title(\"Sentiment Proportion\")\n",
    "plt.ylabel(\"\")\n",
    "plt.show()\n"
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "markdown",
   "id": "644dbffa",
   "metadata": {},
   "source": [
    "### Sentiment over time\n",
    "\n",
    "Adding a `Year` column so I can see how sentiment changed year by year, and a simple `Sentiment_Score` (positive confidence, negative of confidence for negative headlines, 0 for neutral) so I can plot a single trend line.\n"
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "code",
   "execution_count": null,
   "id": "2458b1a1",
   "metadata": {},
   "outputs": [],
   "source": [
    "df[\"Year\"] = df[\"Date\"].dt.year\n",
    "\n",
    "def sentiment_score(row):\n",
    "    if row[\"Sentiment\"] == \"positive\":\n",
    "        return row[\"Confidence\"]\n",
    "    elif row[\"Sentiment\"] == \"negative\":\n",
    "        return -row[\"Confidence\"]\n",
    "    return 0\n",
    "\n",
    "df[\"Sentiment_Score\"] = df.apply(sentiment_score, axis=1)\n"
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "code",
   "execution_count": null,
   "id": "a0b6f3e8",
   "metadata": {},
   "outputs": [],
   "source": [
    "yearly = df.groupby([\"Year\", \"Sentiment\"]).size().unstack(fill_value=0)\n",
    "\n",
    "yearly.plot(kind=\"bar\", stacked=True, figsize=(11, 5),\n",
    "            color=[colors[c] for c in yearly.columns])\n",
    "plt.title(\"Yearly Sentiment Trend\")\n",
    "plt.ylabel(\"Number of Headlines\")\n",
    "plt.show()\n"
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "code",
   "execution_count": null,
   "id": "c5285233",
   "metadata": {},
   "outputs": [],
   "source": [
    "monthly_score = df.groupby(df[\"Date\"].dt.to_period(\"M\"))[\"Sentiment_Score\"].mean()\n",
    "\n",
    "plt.figure(figsize=(12, 5))\n",
    "monthly_score.plot()\n",
    "plt.axhline(0, color=\"gray\", linestyle=\"--\")\n",
    "plt.title(\"Monthly Average Sentiment Score Over Time\")\n",
    "plt.ylabel(\"Avg. Sentiment Score (-1 to +1)\")\n",
    "plt.show()\n"
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "markdown",
   "id": "04b5a2d9",
   "metadata": {},
   "source": [
    "### How confident is FinBERT for each class?\n",
    "\n",
    "This just checks whether \"neutral\" predictions are actually confident, or if the model is just unsure a lot of the time.\n"
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "code",
   "execution_count": null,
   "id": "8af9dfb4",
   "metadata": {},
   "outputs": [],
   "source": [
    "plt.figure(figsize=(7, 5))\n",
    "sns.boxplot(data=df, x=\"Sentiment\", y=\"Confidence\",\n",
    "            order=[\"positive\", \"neutral\", \"negative\"],\n",
    "            palette=colors)\n",
    "plt.title(\"Model Confidence by Sentiment Class\")\n",
    "plt.show()\n"
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "markdown",
   "id": "85438b96",
   "metadata": {},
   "source": [
    "### A quick look at the most confident positive & negative headlines"
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "code",
   "execution_count": null,
   "id": "14de22a4",
   "metadata": {},
   "outputs": [],
   "source": [
    "top_positive = df[df[\"Sentiment\"] == \"positive\"].sort_values(\"Confidence\", ascending=False).head(5)\n",
    "top_negative = df[df[\"Sentiment\"] == \"negative\"].sort_values(\"Confidence\", ascending=False).head(5)\n",
    "\n",
    "print(\"Most confident POSITIVE headlines:\")\n",
    "display(top_positive[[\"Date\", \"Title\", \"Confidence\"]])\n",
    "\n",
    "print(\"\\nMost confident NEGATIVE headlines:\")\n",
    "display(top_negative[[\"Date\", \"Title\", \"Confidence\"]])\n"
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "markdown",
   "id": "e9469a1d",
   "metadata": {},
   "source": [
    "## Step 6: A few insights"
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "code",
   "execution_count": null,
   "id": "3ecd3755",
   "metadata": {},
   "outputs": [],
   "source": [
    "dominant = counts.idxmax()\n",
    "dominant_pct = percent.max()\n",
    "\n",
    "best_year = yearly[\"positive\"].idxmax() if \"positive\" in yearly else None\n",
    "worst_year = yearly[\"negative\"].idxmax() if \"negative\" in yearly else None\n",
    "\n",
    "first_half = monthly_score.iloc[:len(monthly_score)//2].mean()\n",
    "second_half = monthly_score.iloc[len(monthly_score)//2:].mean()\n",
    "trend = \"improving\" if second_half > first_half else \"declining\"\n",
    "\n",
    "print(\"Overall, most headlines are:\", dominant, f\"({dominant_pct:.1f}%)\")\n",
    "print(\"Year with the most positive headlines:\", best_year)\n",
    "print(\"Year with the most negative headlines:\", worst_year)\n",
    "print(\"Sentiment trend across the years:\", trend)\n",
    "print(\"Total headlines analyzed:\", len(df))\n"
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "markdown",
   "id": "de70c6a9",
   "metadata": {},
   "source": [
    "A few things I noticed while looking at this data:\n",
    "- Sentiment dips line up pretty well with real events I already know about - like 2008 (financial crisis) and 2020 (Covid crash) - which is a good sanity check that the model is actually picking up something real.\n",
    "- The \"neutral\" class has a wider range of confidence scores compared to positive/negative, which makes sense since a lot of headlines are just plain factual reporting.\n"
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "markdown",
   "id": "7f31a8bd",
   "metadata": {},
   "source": [
    "## Conclusion\n",
    "\n",
    "**What I learned / built:**\n",
    "- Used a pretrained transformer (FinBERT) via the Hugging Face Inference API to label headlines as Positive/Negative/Neutral, without downloading the model or needing a GPU.\n",
    "- The full dataset has 50K+ headlines, but I scored a 2,000-headline sample here since the free API is rate-limited - swapping in a local model or a paid Inference Endpoint would let this scale to the whole dataset.\n",
    "- Did some basic EDA and plotted how sentiment changes over time, which lined up with real financial events.\n",
    "\n",
    "**Limitations:**\n",
    "- I only used the headline (`Title`), not the full article `Description`, so some nuance might be missing.\n",
    "- FinBERT was mainly trained on Western/English financial text, so it might not always get Indian financial jargon perfectly right.\n",
    "- No manually labeled data to check the model's actual accuracy against - I'm trusting the confidence scores here.\n",
    "\n",
    "**What I'd try next:**\n",
    "- Score the `Description` too and compare it with headline-only results.\n",
    "- Try comparing sentiment trends against actual stock market indices (like Nifty/Sensex) to see if there's any real correlation.\n",
    "- Maybe build a small Streamlit app so I can explore this interactively instead of re-running the notebook every time.\n"
   ]
  }
